WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.534
-Look at it. It's real.
-It looks like a record.

00:00:03.534 --> 00:00:04.767
-Exactly. Thanks for coming out.

00:00:04.767 --> 00:00:06.033
You're performing the song
tonight.

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:07.567
-Yes, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:10.267
My backup dancers will be here
in just a second.

00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:12.434
-Are you psyched about this?
I mean, look at this.

00:00:12.434 --> 00:00:14.534
It's finally out.
-Yes, I've been working on this

00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:16.767
for a really long time,
and now it's out in the world.

00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:18.367
And I feel like --
I've felt like

00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:20.133
I was 20 months pregnant
with this.

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:22.067
[ Laughter ]
For a very long time.

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:23.367
It's out in the world
and it's great.

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:24.567
And I want everybody
to know the story.

00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:26.033
And also, I think it's terrible

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:27.934
and all I can think about is
what's wrong.

00:00:27.934 --> 00:00:29.367
You know how it is.
You do something,

00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:31.534
and all you can think is like,
"I want it to be perfect."

00:00:31.534 --> 00:00:33.634
-But you were saying as you were
working on this, you went on

00:00:33.634 --> 00:00:36.868
this crazy, awesome train --
kind of -- I guess

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:38.634
you can call it a vacation,
but you were working.

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:40.901
-Yeah, well --
-Well, you can't stop.

00:00:40.901 --> 00:00:44.734
-This is an extended vacation
family plan that Susan and I had

00:00:44.734 --> 00:00:46.234
with both of our families that

00:00:46.234 --> 00:00:47.934
we had to put off twice
because of COVID.

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:49.968
And then finally,
we could all do it.

00:00:49.968 --> 00:00:52.634
And it's mine and Susan's idea
of a fun time,

00:00:52.634 --> 00:00:55.501
which is taking a train trip
that never ends.

00:00:55.501 --> 00:00:58.234
-Yeah. I would think that would
be an amazing trip

00:00:58.234 --> 00:01:00.167
for maybe like three days.

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:01.200
-No, we did it for 10.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.501
-No.
-Yes.

00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:04.501
[ Laughter ]
-You can't leave the train?

00:01:04.501 --> 00:01:07.300
-No. New York.
And then we went to Chicago.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.100
And then we went to New Orleans.
Then we went to San Antonio

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.934
and Austin, all on the train.

00:01:11.934 --> 00:01:14.334
It's fan-fricking-tastic.
-It is cool, right?

00:01:14.334 --> 00:01:18.133
-But you have to like
"going places" and never

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:20.834
"getting anywhere," because you
never get off the --

00:01:20.834 --> 00:01:22.667
You never get off the form of
transportation.

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:24.534
-All your photos are
just blurry trees.

00:01:24.534 --> 00:01:25.767
-Yes, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:28.267
Also, like, "I fell down
in the hall again."

00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:30.667
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, exactly.

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:32.968
And my poor family, I was
working on this the whole time.

00:01:32.968 --> 00:01:35.400
So, like, every time we go
across -- like, we see

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.100
a landmark
or we go across a bridge

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.300
that's named for a person,
I'm like, "I have YouTube clips

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.033
from 1937 from that person
giving a speech.

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:45.767
Everybody, gather around."
-Yeah and they're like,

00:01:45.767 --> 00:01:47.701
"Stop working
Let's just have fun."

00:01:47.701 --> 00:01:51.667
-"Let's play crazy eights,
Rachel. Stop and have a beer."

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:53.834
-Every Monday,
I know you're on MSNBC.

00:01:53.834 --> 00:01:56.367
Everyone is talking about
midterm elections

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:57.934
and why they're important.

00:01:57.934 --> 00:02:00.634
Can we just remind everyone why
midterm elections are important?

00:02:00.634 --> 00:02:02.767
-So, I mean, every election
is important, right?

00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:06.334
But this one is -- this one
feels like a very big deal.

00:02:06.334 --> 00:02:08.000
It's the first election since

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.534
the sort of disastrous election
of 2020.

00:02:10.534 --> 00:02:12.701
-Mm-hmm.
-And "The Washington Post" just

00:02:12.701 --> 00:02:15.501
did a thing the other day where
they said -- they looked at all

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:17.367
the candidates running in
statewide races

00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:19.300
and congressional races
all around the country.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.934
And a numerical majority
of the Republican candidates

00:02:23.934 --> 00:02:26.634
don't believe in
the last election results.

00:02:26.634 --> 00:02:29.267
-Mm.
-So, that's hard because if you

00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:32.467
don't believe elections are the
way we decide who is in power,

00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:35.434
how exactly would you like us
to choose you?

00:02:35.434 --> 00:02:36.868
[ Laughter ]

00:02:36.868 --> 00:02:38.300
It's interesting. Yeah.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.934
It's like, "Yeah, this is an
election, what you're doing."

00:02:39.934 --> 00:02:42.501
-Yes. And so, if you don't think
that elections are how people

00:02:42.501 --> 00:02:44.033
should get into power
in America,

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:46.167
I don't know what you want me
to do for you.

00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:48.534
But I'm disinclined to vote
for you.

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:51.167
-Definitely some interesting
characters this time around.

00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:54.868
-Yeah, there's -- I mean --

00:02:54.868 --> 00:02:56.934
Political science
is not real science.

00:02:56.934 --> 00:02:58.467
Like, it's not physics.
-Yeah.

00:02:58.467 --> 00:03:01.601
-But to the extent that there is
a political science rule

00:03:01.601 --> 00:03:06.100
in America, it is after --
if your party currently has

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.567
a president in the White House,

00:03:07.567 --> 00:03:09.133
the next time
there's an election,

00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:10.334
you're going to get shellacked.

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:11.968
Like, that's basically the rule.

00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:12.901
-It's usually what happens.
Yeah.

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:14.334
-It's almost always what
happens.

00:03:14.334 --> 00:03:16.534
So because we've got a Democrat,
Joe Biden, in the White House,

00:03:16.534 --> 00:03:19.601
that means, in political science
terms, the Democrats

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:23.334
should expect to not do well
in these elections.

00:03:23.334 --> 00:03:27.100
But Herschel Walker, you know?

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:28.801
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:31.834
-I mean, it's just every day you
go, "Oh, my goodness."

00:03:31.834 --> 00:03:36.334
-Yeah. And, I mean, really,
they -- like, the nominee for

00:03:36.334 --> 00:03:39.868
governor in Arizona doesn't
believe in election results.

00:03:39.868 --> 00:03:41.067
-Yeah.
-Like, what then?

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:42.701
I would do a lot of things
with you,

00:03:42.701 --> 00:03:44.601
but not participate in
an election.

00:03:44.601 --> 00:03:46.000
-Yeah, interesting.
-That's the thing.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.167
That's not a thing for you.
-Do you think Trump started

00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:53.501
a whole new type of world
with these candidates?

00:03:53.501 --> 00:03:57.100
-Trump saying that he didn't
lose the last election and still

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:59.167
should be in power and there
should be some other way

00:03:59.167 --> 00:04:03.934
to reinstall him in power is a
thing now in Republican politics

00:04:03.934 --> 00:04:05.033
and it's not a fringe thing.

00:04:05.033 --> 00:04:07.167
It's a majority of
their candidates.

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:08.300
And so, that's, in part, why

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:09.767
this whole election
is really important

00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:12.567
because if one of the two major
parties is going to go

00:04:12.567 --> 00:04:16.133
that direction where elections
aren't how we determine

00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:17.601
the future of political power,

00:04:17.601 --> 00:04:20.434
well, there isn't really
another good choice.

00:04:20.434 --> 00:04:22.300
-Yeah.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.634
-So it makes this important.

00:04:24.634 --> 00:04:27.133
There's also a bunch of other --
you know, there's another --

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.534
a lot of really practical things
that matter in the --

00:04:29.534 --> 00:04:31.000
for this election and the world.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.634
You know, the Democrats and
Biden have been very supportive

00:04:33.634 --> 00:04:36.767
of Ukraine with Russia
waging war on Ukraine.

00:04:36.767 --> 00:04:38.334
It's not at all clear
the Republicans

00:04:38.334 --> 00:04:40.100
would continue doing that.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:42.501
With Roe vs. Wade being
overturned, the more power

00:04:42.501 --> 00:04:44.968
Republicans have, the more bans
on abortion we're going to have.

00:04:44.968 --> 00:04:46.667
And the Republicans in Congress,

00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:49.334
if they get power, they want
a national abortion ban.

00:04:49.334 --> 00:04:51.367
That means every state.
That includes New York.

00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:53.400
That includes California.
That includes everywhere.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.000
So it's just -- this is not
the election to sit out.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.267
-Yeah.
-It's four weeks from tomorrow.

00:04:59.267 --> 00:05:01.567
Whatever your political
preferences are, this is

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:04.100
going to be a really,
really consequential one.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.100
-Yeah, we'll be watching you.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:08.133
Can we talk about "Ultra"?
-Yeah.

00:05:08.133 --> 00:05:09.667
-How did you find this story?

00:05:09.667 --> 00:05:12.100
Tell everyone what it's about.
It's very, very interesting.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.267
-Okay, so you know
we had January 6th.

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:15.701
That was bad.
-Yes.

00:05:15.701 --> 00:05:19.267
-We've had other problems like
that before, and I feel like

00:05:19.267 --> 00:05:21.133
there's something that's
happened over the past like

00:05:21.133 --> 00:05:24.033
seven or eight years
where every time something new

00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:25.634
and terrible happens in
the news, we're like,

00:05:25.634 --> 00:05:28.167
"This is unprecedented.
This has never happened before."

00:05:28.167 --> 00:05:30.067
And that's true
until you look it up.

00:05:30.067 --> 00:05:31.734
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.734 --> 00:05:33.467
There's always some --
-Someone's done it already.

00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:35.133
-Some terrible precedent.

00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:38.701
And so, there's this amazing
case in 1944, where like

00:05:38.701 --> 00:05:40.601
30 people --
more than two dozen people

00:05:40.601 --> 00:05:42.667
all got put on trial
for sedition,

00:05:42.667 --> 00:05:45.200
for trying to violently
overthrow the government.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:48.067
And okay, we just had
an experience like that.

00:05:48.067 --> 00:05:50.334
We've got people on trial
for sedition now. Okay.

00:05:50.334 --> 00:05:54.400
And when it happened in 1944,
in the middle of the trial,

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.968
the judge died
and they all got off.

00:05:56.968 --> 00:05:58.467
-Is that right?
-Yes.

00:05:58.467 --> 00:06:02.567
And it turns out that there was
all these members of Congress

00:06:02.567 --> 00:06:06.000
who really were on the side of
the accused seditionists,

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.501
and they leaned on
the Justice Department.

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:11.234
They told the attorney general
he needed to fire

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:13.267
the main prosecutor, and he did.

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:15.801
Like, that can't be.
You can't do that.

00:06:15.801 --> 00:06:17.334
That's crazy town.

00:06:17.334 --> 00:06:19.234
Turns out there were all these
members of Congress who were,

00:06:19.234 --> 00:06:21.801
like, taking money
from the Hitler government,

00:06:21.801 --> 00:06:23.133
from the actual Nazis.

00:06:23.133 --> 00:06:24.534
Not the metaphorical Nazis.

00:06:24.534 --> 00:06:26.701
The N-N-Nazis. The real Nazis.

00:06:26.701 --> 00:06:28.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.100
And they were going to get
in trouble for it.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.100
And then they were like, "No,
shut down the investigation."

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.267
And it did get shut down.

00:06:33.267 --> 00:06:34.934
It's bananas.

00:06:34.934 --> 00:06:37.501
-How do you find all this stuff
and how do you know about this?

00:06:37.501 --> 00:06:39.767
Deep dive.
-I live in a dark place.

00:06:39.767 --> 00:06:41.133
-Yeah, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:41.133 --> 00:06:43.534
You go in there and you find
these amazing stories.

00:06:43.534 --> 00:06:45.868
And how many podcasts are there
for the whole series?

00:06:45.868 --> 00:06:48.701
-It's an eight-episode series.

00:06:48.701 --> 00:06:51.400
And we just dropped
the first two episodes today.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.167
It's free. You can get it
anywhere you get your podcasts.

00:06:53.167 --> 00:06:56.000
-Yes!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.000
-Like, I've been dying to tell
this story.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.234
And so, it's finally getting
out there.

00:07:01.234 --> 00:07:03.434
And I'm making myself
absolutely crazy about it.

00:07:03.434 --> 00:07:04.701
But I hope people
dig it as much.

00:07:04.701 --> 00:07:07.400
-It's out. Congratulations.
"Ultra," everybody.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:09.701
Check it out wherever you listen
to your podcasts.

00:07:09.701 --> 00:07:13.300
"Rachel Maddow Show," Mondays
at 9:00 p.m. on MSNBC.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:14.934
Thank you so much.
Always good to see you.

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:17.133
We'll be right back with more
"Tonight Show." Come on back.

